<div class="callout callout--concept"><span class="callout__icon" aria-hidden="true">💡</span><div class="callout__content">
<p><strong>Self-study quiz.</strong> This quiz is for your own review — answers are not recorded. You’ll get instant feedback on each question.</p>
</div></div>

<div class="quiz"><div class="quiz__top-bar"><div class="quiz__score-tracker" style="display:none;"></div><button class="quiz__start-over" type="button" style="display:none;">Start Over</button></div>
  <div class="quiz__question" data-correct="q1b">
    <div class="quiz__question-text"><span class="quiz__number">1</span> In this course, criticism means</div>
    <ul class="quiz__choices">
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q1" value="q1a" id="q1a">
        <label for="q1a">rating a work from one to five stars.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q1" value="q1b" id="q1b">
        <label for="q1b">the disciplined practice of paying attention to a work of art and saying something informed about it.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q1" value="q1c" id="q1c">
        <label for="q1c">repeating what experts have said.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q1" value="q1d" id="q1d">
        <label for="q1d">finding fault with a work.</label>
      </li>
    </ul>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--correct" style="display:none;">Correct. Criticism can be positive, negative, or neither. What defines it is disciplined attention and informed statements.</div>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--incorrect" style="display:none;">In the humanities, criticism does not mean faultfinding. It means careful attention plus an informed statement about the work.</div>
  </div>
  <div class="quiz__question" data-correct="q2b">
    <div class="quiz__question-text"><span class="quiz__number">2</span> Descriptive criticism focuses on</div>
    <ul class="quiz__choices">
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q2" value="q2a" id="q2a">
        <label for="q2a">the work's meaning.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q2" value="q2b" id="q2b">
        <label for="q2b">the formal elements of a work.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q2" value="q2c" id="q2c">
        <label for="q2c">the work's quality.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q2" value="q2d" id="q2d">
        <label for="q2d">the artist's biography.</label>
      </li>
    </ul>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--correct" style="display:none;">Correct. Descriptive criticism asks what is there: line, color, rhythm, structure. It deliberately holds back meaning and judgment.</div>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--incorrect" style="display:none;">Description is the first step. It reports what is actually in the work without yet saying what it means or how good it is.</div>
  </div>
  <div class="quiz__question" data-correct="q3a">
    <div class="quiz__question-text"><span class="quiz__number">3</span> Interpretive criticism centers on</div>
    <ul class="quiz__choices">
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q3" value="q3a" id="q3a">
        <label for="q3a">the content: what the work means or expresses.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q3" value="q3b" id="q3b">
        <label for="q3b">only the formal elements.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q3" value="q3c" id="q3c">
        <label for="q3c">the cost of the materials.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q3" value="q3d" id="q3d">
        <label for="q3d">whether the work deserves to be in a museum.</label>
      </li>
    </ul>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--correct" style="display:none;">Correct. The formula in this unit is that interpretation explains how form transforms subject matter into content.</div>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--incorrect" style="display:none;">Interpretation moves from what is there to what it means. It should always be grounded in specific observations.</div>
  </div>
  <div class="quiz__question" data-correct="q4d">
    <div class="quiz__question-text"><span class="quiz__number">4</span> Evaluative criticism centers on</div>
    <ul class="quiz__choices">
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q4" value="q4a" id="q4a">
        <label for="q4a">how popular the work is.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q4" value="q4b" id="q4b">
        <label for="q4b">the history of the artist.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q4" value="q4c" id="q4c">
        <label for="q4c">the formal elements only.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q4" value="q4d" id="q4d">
        <label for="q4d">the relative merits of a work.</label>
      </li>
    </ul>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--correct" style="display:none;">Correct. Evaluation asks how good a work is. It relies on description and interpretation first, which is why the order matters.</div>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--incorrect" style="display:none;">Evaluation is about merit: how good a work is and why. It is the third step, after description and interpretation.</div>
  </div>
  <div class="quiz__question" data-correct="q5b">
    <div class="quiz__question-text"><span class="quiz__number">5</span> Which set lists the three standards for evaluation presented in this unit?</div>
    <ul class="quiz__choices">
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q5" value="q5a" id="q5a">
        <label for="q5a">Popularity, price, and fame.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q5" value="q5b" id="q5b">
        <label for="q5b">Perfection, insight, and inexhaustibility.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q5" value="q5c" id="q5c">
        <label for="q5c">Beauty, realism, and age.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q5" value="q5d" id="q5d">
        <label for="q5d">Originality, speed, and size.</label>
      </li>
    </ul>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--correct" style="display:none;">Correct. A strong work is unified (perfection), reveals something meaningful about human experience (insight), and rewards repeated attention (inexhaustibility).</div>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--incorrect" style="display:none;">The three standards are perfection (everything contributes), insight (it reveals something meaningful), and inexhaustibility (it rewards return visits).</div>
  </div>
  <div class="quiz__question" data-correct="q6a">
    <div class="quiz__question-text"><span class="quiz__number">6</span> Leonardo's Last Supper is organized around a single vanishing point behind Christ's head, with the apostles in four groups of three. What kind of relationship is this?</div>
    <ul class="quiz__choices">
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q6" value="q6a" id="q6a">
        <label for="q6a">A structural relationship: the principle that governs how the parts fit the whole.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q6" value="q6b" id="q6b">
        <label for="q6b">An interpretation of the painting's religious meaning.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q6" value="q6c" id="q6c">
        <label for="q6c">A detail relationship between two brushstrokes.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q6" value="q6d" id="q6d">
        <label for="q6d">A purely evaluative judgment.</label>
      </li>
    </ul>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--correct" style="display:none;">Correct. The vanishing point and the four groups are not small details or one region; they are the structure that organizes the whole composition.</div>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--incorrect" style="display:none;">Descriptive criticism has three levels: detail, regional, and structural. The organizing principle for the whole painting is structural.</div>
  </div>
  <div class="quiz__question" data-correct="q7d">
    <div class="quiz__question-text"><span class="quiz__number">7</span> The statement that Picasso's Guernica is a better painting than Munch's The Scream is</div>
    <ul class="quiz__choices">
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q7" value="q7a" id="q7a">
        <label for="q7a">historical.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q7" value="q7b" id="q7b">
        <label for="q7b">interpretive.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q7" value="q7c" id="q7c">
        <label for="q7c">descriptive.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q7" value="q7d" id="q7d">
        <label for="q7d">evaluative.</label>
      </li>
    </ul>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--correct" style="display:none;">Correct. Saying one work is better than another is a judgment of merit. Evaluation needs a stated standard to be convincing.</div>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--incorrect" style="display:none;">Notice the word better. It judges merit, and judging merit is the job of evaluative criticism.</div>
  </div>
  <div class="quiz__question" data-correct="q8c">
    <div class="quiz__question-text"><span class="quiz__number">8</span> The statement that the bull in Guernica symbolizes the cruelty and indifference of the regime that ordered the bombing is</div>
    <ul class="quiz__choices">
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q8" value="q8a" id="q8a">
        <label for="q8a">descriptive.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q8" value="q8b" id="q8b">
        <label for="q8b">purely historical.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q8" value="q8c" id="q8c">
        <label for="q8c">interpretive.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q8" value="q8d" id="q8d">
        <label for="q8d">evaluative.</label>
      </li>
    </ul>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--correct" style="display:none;">Correct. It says what a detail of the painting stands for. Good interpretations point to specific details and explain how the form creates the meaning.</div>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--incorrect" style="display:none;">The statement explains what something stands for, not just what is there or how good it is. That is interpretation.</div>
  </div>
  <div class="quiz__question" data-correct="q9a">
    <div class="quiz__question-text"><span class="quiz__number">9</span> Noticing that the sky in Munch's The Scream is painted in long, swirling bands of orange that echo the curves of the figure's body is</div>
    <ul class="quiz__choices">
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q9" value="q9a" id="q9a">
        <label for="q9a">descriptive criticism.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q9" value="q9b" id="q9b">
        <label for="q9b">not criticism at all.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q9" value="q9c" id="q9c">
        <label for="q9c">evaluative criticism.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q9" value="q9d" id="q9d">
        <label for="q9d">interpretive criticism.</label>
      </li>
    </ul>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--correct" style="display:none;">Correct. It reports formal elements and the relationships between them without saying what they mean or whether they succeed.</div>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--incorrect" style="display:none;">The statement reports what is there (swirling bands, echoing curves). It does not say what they mean or whether they are good, so it is descriptive.</div>
  </div>
  <div class="quiz__question" data-correct="q10c">
    <div class="quiz__question-text"><span class="quiz__number">10</span> Klimt's The Kiss rewards repeated looking: you keep finding the contrast of geometric and organic patterns or the danger of the cliff edge. Which evaluative standard does this illustrate?</div>
    <ul class="quiz__choices">
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q10" value="q10a" id="q10a">
        <label for="q10a">Popularity.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q10" value="q10b" id="q10b">
        <label for="q10b">Perfection only.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q10" value="q10c" id="q10c">
        <label for="q10c">Inexhaustibility.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q10" value="q10d" id="q10d">
        <label for="q10d">Historical importance.</label>
      </li>
    </ul>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--correct" style="display:none;">Correct. A work that keeps revealing something new each time you return to it is inexhaustible, and that is a mark of a strong work.</div>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--incorrect" style="display:none;">The key phrase is keeps revealing something new. That is the standard of inexhaustibility, the quality of resisting becoming stale.</div>
  </div>
</div>

